  "source": "SECTION 5: FIRE-FIGHTING MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "Yes, do not use direct water stream to extinguish fires. Do not release runoff from fire control methods to sewers or waterways.",
  "source": "SECTION 5: FIRE-FIGHTING MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "Use appropriate personal protective equipment, avoid breathing vapors, mist or gas, avoid contact with spilled material, ensure adequate ventilation, remove all sources of ignition, use non-sparking tools and equipment.",
  "source": "SECTION 6: ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "For safe handling and storage, use appropriate personal protective equipment, avoid breathing vapors, mist, or gas, avoid contact with spilled material, ensure adequate ventilation, remove all sources of ignition, and use non-sparking tools and equipment.",
  "source": "SECTION 6: ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "Engineering controls should be such that adequate ventilation is provided, provide general or local exhaust ventilation systems to maintain airborne concentrations below OSHA PELs. Local exhaust ventilation is preferred because it prevents contaminant dispersion into the work place by controlling it at its source. Respiratory protection: seek professional advice prior to respirator selection and use. Follow OSHA respirator regulations (29 CFR 1910.134) and, if necessary, wear a MSHA / NIOSH approved respirator. Select respirator based on its suitability to provide adequate worker protection for given working conditions, level of airborne contamination, and presence of sufficient oxygen. For emergency or non-routine operations (e.g. cleaning spills, reactor vessels, or storage tanks), wear an SCBA. Wear protective eyeglasses or chemical safety goggles, per OSHA eye-and face-protection regulations (29 CFR 1910.133). Contact lenses are not eye protective devices. Appropriate eye protection must be worn instead of, or in conjunction with, contact lenses. Wear chemically protective gloves, boots, aprons and gauntlets to prevent prolonged or repeated skin contact.",
  "source": "SECTION 8: EXPOSURE CONTROLS / PERSONAL PROTECTION"
}

{
  "answer": "The product is a liquid with a yellow to amber appearance, mild odor, and a flash point of >275°F (>135°C). It has a relative density of 8.33 ??9.02 lb/gal, specific gravity of 1.000 ??1.070, and is soluble in water. The initial boiling point is 480°F (248.9°C), and it has a vapor density >1.",
  "source": "SECTION 9: PHYSICAL AND CHEMICAL PROPERTIES"
}

{
  "answer": "The product is stable under normal handling conditions, but thermal oxidative decomposition can produce carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, and unknown organic compounds.",
  "source": "SECTION 10: STABILITY AND REACTIVITY"
}

{
  "answer": "Carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, and unknown organic compounds",
  "source": "SECTION 10: STABILITY AND REACTIVITY"
}

{
  "answer": "The toxicological information available for the product includes Acute toxicity, dermal (Category 5), Acute toxicity, oral (Category 4), Skin Irritation (Category 3), Serious eye damage (Category 1), Reproductive toxicity (Category 2). The Hazard Statements include H302 Harmful if swallowed, H313 May be harmful in contact with skin, H316 Causes mild skin irritation, H318 Causes serious eye damage, and H361 Suspected of damaging fertility or the unborn child.",
  "source": "Hazard Classification, GHS Classification"
}

{
  "answer": "To use this product safely, follow these precautions and handling instructions: Wear full protective clothing and NIOSH-approved self-contained breathing apparatus with full face piece operated in the pressure demand or other positive breathing mode when fighting fires. When handling the product, use appropriate personal protective equipment, avoid breathing vapors, mist or gas, and avoid contact with spilled material. Ensure adequate ventilation and remove all sources of ignition. Use non-sparking tools and equipment. For accidental release measures, use standard work uniform, impervious gloves, safety glasses, and increase PPE level as deemed necessary in any given situation. In case of fire, evacuate the area, do not use direct water stream to extinguish fires, and do not release runoff from fire control methods to sewers or waterways.",
  "source": "SECTION 6: ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES, SECTION 5: FIRE-FIGHTING MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "The potential hazards associated with NAPA DOT 3 Brake Fluid include Target Organ Effect, Harmful by ingestion, Irritant, Teratogen, Reproductive hazard according to OSHA Hazards, and Acute toxicity, dermal (Category 5), Acute toxicity, oral (Category 4), Skin Irritation (Category 3), Serious eye damage (Category 1), Reproductive toxicity (Category 2) according to GHS Classification. The Hazard Statements are H302 Harmful if swallowed, H313 May be harmful in contact with skin, H316 Causes mild skin irritation, H318 Causes serious eye damage, and H361 Suspected of damaging fertility or the unborn child.",
  "source": "SECTION 2: HAZARD(S) IDENTIFICATION, SECTION 4: FIRST AID MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "The product is a Health Hazard, with specific health effects or risks associated with exposure including oral toxicity (LD50 >2,000 mg/kg for Polyalkylene Glycol Monobutyl Ether), dermal toxicity (LD50 >2,000 mg/kg for Polyalkylene Glycol Monobutyl Ether), and potential carcinogenicity (no component identified as a known or anticipated carcinogen by NTP, probable, possible, or confirmed human carcinogen by IARC, or carcinogen or potential carcinogen by OSHA).",
  "source": "EXPOSURE GUIDELINES, LISTED CARCINOGEN"
}

{
  "answer": "Remove victim to fresh air. If not breathing, give artificial respiration. If breathing is difficult, give oxygen. Consult a physician.",
  "source": "SECTION 4: FIRST AID MEASURE"
}

{
  "answer": "Wear impervious gloves, safety glasses, and increase PPE level as deemed appropriate in any given situation.",
  "source": "SECTION 6: ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "In case of eye contact with the product, flush eyes with plenty of water. If irritation persists, seek medical attention.",
  "source": "SECTION 5: FIRE FIGHTING MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "In case of ingestion, rinse mouth with water. Consult a physician.",
  "source": "SECTION 4: FIRST AID MEASURES"
}

{
  "answer": "Whatever cannot be saved for recovery or recycling should be disposed of in an approved waste facility in accordance with Federal, State/Provincial and Local requirements.",
  "source": "SECTION 13: DISPOSAL CONSIDERATIONS"
}

{
  "answer": "The product should be stored in a well-ventilated area, away from direct sunlight and heat sources. It is recommended to store the product in its original container, tightly sealed, and kept upright.",
  "source": "SECTION 7: HANDLING AND STORAGE"
}

