{
  "answer": "Yes, the product is stable under normal handling conditions (stable at room temperature in closed containers). However, thermal oxidative decomposition can produce carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, and unknown organic compounds.",
  "source": "SECTION 10: STABILITY AND REACTIVITY"
}

{
  "answer": "Engineering controls should be such that adequate ventilation is provided, and local exhaust ventilation systems are preferred to maintain airborne concentrations below OSHA PELs. Personal protective equipment includes standard work uniform, impervious gloves, safety glasses, and increasing the level of PPE as deemed appropriate in any given situation. Respiratory protection requires seeking professional advice prior to respirator selection and use, following OSHA respirator regulations (29 CFR 1910.134), and wearing a MSHA / NIOSH approved respirator, specifically an SCBA for emergency or non-routine operations.",
  "source": "SECTION 8: EXPOSURE CONTROLS / PERSONAL PROTECTION"
}

{
  "answer": "Whatever cannot be saved for recovery or recycling should be disposed of in an approved waste facility in accordance with Federal, State/Provincial and Local requirements. Whenever possible, material should not be allowed to enter sewage disposal systems.",
  "source": "SECTION 13: DISPOSAL CONSIDERATIONS"
}

